# BRIEFR UI design-system rules

Authoritative sources (read before UI work):
- Rules/SSOT: `docs/design/design-system.md`
- Tokens: `frontend/src/styles/tokens.css`
- Rationale: `docs/decisions/ADR-003-ui-design-system.md`
- Roadmap: `docs/planning/ui-modernization-plan.md`

These rules apply to ALL generated or edited UI code. If a change conflicts with them, stop
and reconcile with `docs/design/design-system.md` (which wins over ad-hoc styling; runtime
truth in `docs/PRODUCT_STATUS.md` wins over planning docs).

## Tokens (hard rules)
- NEVER hardcode colors. Use semantic tokens (`--surface-*`, `--text-*`, `--severity-*`,
  `--status-*`, `--accent-*`). No raw hex, `rgb()`, or the underlying `--c-*` primitives in
  component code.
- NEVER hardcode spacing, radius, font-size, z-index, or motion durations. Use `--space-*`,
  `--radius-*`, `--font-size-*`, `--z-*`, `--motion-*`.
- ALWAYS use semantic token names; never encode a literal color in a name (no `--orange`).

## Color semantics
- Red (`--danger`, `--severity-critical`, `--status-error`) is ONLY for destructive actions,
  critical severity, and error status. NEVER use red for neutral links, selection, or toggles.
- "Selected/active" state ALWAYS uses `--accent-selected` (nav, sidebar, chips, rows, radios).
  Do not invent a per-surface active color.
- Severity uses the `--severity-*` scale AND a card left-accent; never rely on color alone —
  pair with a label/icon.

## Components
- PREFER existing reusable components; do not duplicate a pattern that already has a primitive.
- PREFER Radix UI primitives (Checkbox, RadioGroup, Switch, Select, Slider, Tabs, Tooltip,
  Popover, Dialog/AlertDialog, DropdownMenu, ScrollArea) styled with BRIEFR tokens.
- NEVER use native `<input type="checkbox">`, `<input type="radio">`, or `<select>` — use the
  primitives (native defaults are a known "amateur" defect).
- Tooltips/popovers MUST be portaled and collision-aware. Tables MUST use the shared
  `DataGrid` (`table-layout: fixed` + shared `<col>` so resize keeps header/body aligned).
- Charts MUST live in a fixed-height wrapper (`maintainAspectRatio:false`) and render an
  EmptyState when data is empty/zero — never allow a chart to grow the page unbounded.
- Every async region implements four states: loading (skeleton), empty, error (+ request-id),
  data. Empty and error MUST be visually distinct.
- Follow the design system BEFORE introducing a new pattern; new patterns require a
  `design-system.md` update in the same PR.

## Accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA minimum)
- Maintain AA contrast (≥ 4.5:1 body text; ≥ 3:1 large/bold and UI boundaries). Use
  `--text-muted` (AA-guaranteed); do not dim text below it.
- Every interactive element shows the standard `--focus-ring` (2px, accent). Never remove
  outlines without an equal-or-better replacement.
- Icon-only controls (bell, "…", pin, close-X) MUST have an `aria-label`.
- Interactive targets ≥ 24px; primary buttons ≥ 30px. Logical tab order; no focus traps;
  modals trap focus and restore it on close; Esc closes overlays.
- Clickable elements must look clickable (hover + cursor); non-interactive cards must not.

## Motion
- RESPECT `prefers-reduced-motion` and the tool-wide motion toggle (`data-motion` on `<html>`).
- Keep transitions between 120–180ms (`--motion-fast`/`--motion-normal`), `--motion-slow`
  (220ms) only for drawer/modal, unless justified in the PR.
- Animate ONLY `transform` and `opacity` (GPU-friendly). Never animate layout properties
  (width/height/top/left). No infinite/decorative animation.

## Identity & scope
- PRESERVE the BRIEFR dark terminal identity: keep `--accent-primary` (#c8b88a), mono labels,
  density-first layout (fill width; `max-width` for prose only; no large centered columns).
- DO NOT introduce Tailwind (or any CSS/component framework) unless the maintainer explicitly
  requests it (ADR-003). shadcn may be used only as a pattern reference, adapted to BRIEFR CSS.
- Keep components composable and reusable (Primitives → Composites → Features); do not
  re-implement a lower layer inside a feature.
